# lws_cancel_service is libwebsockets' one documented thread-safe entry point:
# its cross-thread wake-up is synchronized by a pipe write (a syscall), which is
# real synchronization TSan's memory model cannot see. Everything else in lws
# runs single-threaded on its owning shard.
race:lws_cancel_service

# lws's _realloc keeps a global `allocated` byte counter via
# malloc_usable_size (lib/core/alloc.c) with no synchronisation. It exists only
# where glibc provides malloc_usable_size, which is why this is Linux-only. It
# is read by nothing — pure allocation statistics — so concurrent shard threads
# racing on it cannot affect behaviour. Contrast the log-context refcount and
# lifecycle tags, which ARE load-bearing and are fixed properly by building lws
# with LWS_MAX_SMP > 1 (see net/CMakeLists.txt).
race:_realloc

# lws takes its context-refcount mutex and its per-thread mutex in opposite
# orders on the context-create path (lws_create_context -> lws_wsi_inject_to_loop)
# and the wsi-close path, so TSan reports a lock cycle whenever one context is
# created while another context's service thread runs. Production closes that
# window by construction: ShardPool creates every context before starting any
# thread and joins every thread before destroying any context. The test harness
# deliberately interleaves independent client and server contexts, which is what
# trips this. Revisit if lws fixes its lock ordering upstream.
deadlock:lws_mutex_refcount_lock
